Issue: The harness answers from the memory's demonstrated examples, but the channel it delivered them through is taken off the conversation at 20,000 tokens — 80,000 characters at four characters to the token — while the question itself is only taken off at 50,000 tokens, 200,000 characters. The parent run's LawBench memory is 105,318 characters, so 99 of its 100 samples were handed a five-line preview and a file path instead of the demonstration set and the agent went paging for it (read_file 0 -> 71, grep 0 -> 6); the same run paid a retrieve_examples call on every attempt of every task (249 USPTO, 378 Symptom2Disease, 304 LawBench of 2210 calls), so a draw cost two model calls and part of the sample's 32-call budget went on fetching what the question could have carried.

Modification: the demonstration set now travels with the question. `solver/tools/retrieval.py` renders the memory's complete set, closest example first by shared wording, for the prompt, sized to the room that channel has left once the question is in it — min(0.25 * context_window, 195,000 - question) characters, against the 80,000 the tool-result path allowed; `solver/solver.md` carries it as `{examples}` ahead of the question; `solver/tools/__init__.py` registers no tool; `agent.py` builds the agent with no business tools, so an attempt is a single model call; and the skill's first instruction states that the set is included above and nothing else is to be fetched. Grounding, the vote, panel size, panel budget and memory preparation are untouched.

Results: {"evaluation_result": {"USPTO": 0.3167, "Symptom2Disease": 0.9, "LawBench": 0.51, "average": 0.5756, "status": "partial"}, "message": "Delivery itself was achieved and paid off where the set fits: the complete set sat in the question of every sample, no tool was called anywhere in the run (memory_context_chars 0.0, tool_calls empty on all 260 samples), a draw became one model call (2210 -> 993 calls), and the average rose to 0.5756 against the parent's 0.57 — USPTO 0.25 -> 0.3167 (tied with the lineage's best) and Symptom2Disease 0.89 -> 0.9 (the lineage's best), both with shallower panels than the parent's. The expected LawBench gain did not arrive: 7 of its 100 samples were rejected by the provider's input moderation before any model call (DataInspectionFailed on the 105,318-character question, non-retryable), and among the 93 that ran LawBench scored 51 (0.548) against the parent's 57/100 (0.57) — 0.543 against the parent's 0.533 over the 46 examples both runs could attempt — so LawBench's 0.57 -> 0.51 is the samples the provider refused, not the demonstrations, and the demonstrations themselves still do not move that task."}

